<section class="story-section" id="problem">
  <p class="eyebrow">01 / The problem before</p>
  <h2>A likely answer.<br />But a useful one?</h2>
  <p>Predicting the next piece of text teaches a model to continue writing. It does not directly teach it to answer your question helpfully. A plausible continuation might ignore an instruction, invent a fact, or keep rambling.</p>
  <p>What if people could show it good answers, then compare alternatives?</p>
</section>
<section class="story-section" id="idea">
  <p class="eyebrow">02 / The big idea</p>
  <h2>Show. Compare.<br />Practise.</h2>
  <p>In <strong>InstructGPT (2022)</strong>, Long Ouyang and colleagues started with GPT-3. First, people wrote example answers. <strong>Supervised fine-tuning</strong> means further training a model to imitate examples; this gave it a starting style of helpful behaviour.</p>
  <p>Next, people ranked generated answers to the same prompt. A separate <strong>reward model</strong> learned to predict their preferences with a score. Finally, the answer-writing model practised generating responses that earned higher scores. That is <strong>reinforcement learning from human feedback</strong>, or RLHF: using rewards learned from people to guide training.</p>
  <p>People did not rate every practice answer. The scorer supplied feedback at scale. But it learned the preferences of particular people, under particular instructions—not a universal definition of “good.”</p>
