<section class="story-section" id="mattered">
  <p class="eyebrow">04 / Why it mattered</p>
  <h2>The training target<br />moves closer to you.</h2>
  <p>InstructGPT made human judgements part of training for many written requests. Better scores still did not guarantee truthful or safe answers.</p>
  <p>Next, this approach meets a conversational interface: the ChatGPT moment.</p>
  <aside class="chapter-companions"><h3>Also in this chapter</h3><p><strong>Stiennon et al. (2020)</strong> used comparisons to improve summaries. <strong>FLAN</strong> and <strong>T0 (2021)</strong> trained on many tasks written as instructions, showing another route toward following requests: supervised examples.</p></aside>
  <a class="next-chapter" href="/chapters/chatgpt/"><span>Next · 2022</span><strong>The ChatGPT moment ↗</strong><span>What changes when a language model becomes a conversation?</span></a>
</section>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="deeper">
  <summary>Go deeper <span>Preference learning, PPO, and whose preferences count</span></summary>
  <div class="disclosure-body">
    <h3>One comparison, one score difference</h3>
    <p>Let x be a prompt, y+ the preferred response, and y− the other response. The reward model rθ assigns each prompt–response pair a number. Its training loss is <code>L(θ) = −E[log σ(rθ(x, y+) − rθ(x, y−))]</code>, where σ(z) = 1/(1 + exp(−z)) turns a score difference into a predicted preference probability. E means an average over training comparisons. Lower loss encourages a higher score for the preferred answer. The absolute score is not a probability of truth.</p>
    <h3>The scorer and the writer do different jobs</h3>
    <p>The answer-writing model, called a policy, used GPT-3’s decoder-only Transformer architecture at 1.3, 6, or 175 billion parameters—the numbers learned during training. A separate 6-billion-parameter reward model had a scalar output head: one score rather than next-token probabilities. The same reward model was used across policy sizes. Rankings of several responses supplied preferred/rejected pairs.</p>
    <h3>Learning to earn rewards without drifting too far</h3>
    <p><strong>Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO)</strong> is the reinforcement-learning algorithm used to update the policy. Generated responses receive a learned reward, with a per-token penalty for moving away from the supervised starting policy. A compact expression for the reward-and-penalty objective is:</p>
    <p><code>J(φ) = E[x∼D, y∼πφ(·|x)][rθ(x, y) − β log(πφ(y | x) / πSFT(y | x))]</code></p>
    <p>D is the set of training prompts, πφ is the current policy, πSFT the supervised reference, and β controls the penalty. In expectation, the log ratio is a <strong>KL divergence</strong>: a measure of how much one probability distribution differs from another. The reward model and reference remain fixed during this stage; the policy changes. This expression describes the target, not PPO’s clipped update rule. The paper’s PPO-ptx variant also mixes in an objective for predicting pretraining text, reducing several losses on public language tasks.</p>
    <h3>A smaller model people preferred</h3>
    <p>On the paper’s held-out API prompts, evaluators preferred outputs from 1.3-billion-parameter InstructGPT over 175-billion-parameter GPT-3. That demonstrates the value of this training for these requests and evaluators; it does not mean the smaller model surpassed GPT-3 on every capability. The study used mostly English prompts and preferences shaped by its contractors and researcher guidance.</p>
    <h3>A learned reward is still a proxy</h3>
    <p>A proxy is a stand-in for what you actually want. Stiennon and colleagues found that pushing their summary reward model too far could increase its score while human judgements of quality fell. InstructGPT also retained fabricated answers, bias and failures to follow instructions. Agreement with a scorer, agreement with a group of raters, and correctness are different things.</p>
    <h3>Inside this toy</h3>
    <p>Each answer has two hand-assigned features (0-or-1 traits): short length and everyday wording. The scorer is <code>r(y) = wshort · fshort(y) + weveryday · feveryday(y)</code>. It starts at zero and fits the pairwise logistic loss from the selected comparisons, plus <code>0.1 · ||w||² / 2</code> to keep weights small. Changing a choice refits from zero, so clicks do not secretly accumulate extra labels.</p>
    <p>The final bars use <code>p(y) = exp(s · r(y)) / Σz exp(s · r(z))</code> over three fixed answers, starting from equal chances. s is the slider value. This illustrates shifting probability toward reward; it does not implement PPO, generate text, or reproduce InstructGPT. The true and false short answers have identical visible features, so no choice can separate them. Feature labels and the false-answer annotation were written for this illustration, not inferred by the scorer.</p>
  </div>
</details>
